yeah what's up  oh that's easy Iranian oh right  Israeli oh oh right okay I got it now  Sardinian hello everyone welcome to the  Laing focused Channel and my name is  Paul today's topic is the Italian  language Italian may not get as much  attention as French Spanish and  Portuguese because it's mainly spoken in  Europe rather than in former colonies  but it's still one of the most widely  spoken languages in the European Union  with around 68 million speakers they're  around 60 million of those speakers are  in Italy of course where Italian is the  official language Italian is also the  official language of San Marino a tiny  enclaves surrounded by Italy and it's  also a de facto official language of the  Vatican City it's also one of the four  official languages of Switzerland where  it's mainly used in Ticino and the  southern canton of called London and  it's official at the regional level in  Slovenian Astoria and in istria county  in Croatia it's also spoken by overseas  communities among them in the United  States where over 700,000 people speak  it according to the US Census Bureau and  Italian has had a cultural impact on the  rest of the world because Italian  composer's developed our current system  of musical notation Italian is the  common language of musical notation and  nomenclature words like acapella  crescendo soprano and tempo all come  from Italian and if you're a coffee  lover you probably know some Italian  words because Italian coffee culture has  inspired imitation throughout the world  words like espresso from cafe espresso  meaning pressed out coffee grande which  means large venti which means 20 as in  more than 20 ounces of coffee and  treinta which means heart attack  actually it means 30 but yeah Italian is  a member of the Italo Dalmatian branch  of the Romance language family but when  we talk about the Italian language what  exactly are we talking about as I spoke  about at length in my  Oh on the languages of Italy Italy has  historically had an extremely diverse  variety of romance dialects which can be  grouped together and arguably classified  as dialects of various regional  languages the majority of these dialects  belong to the Italo Dalmatian branch but  many in northern Italy do not when we  speak about the Italian language in its  strictest sense we are speaking about  standard Italian which developed from a  specific set of Italo Dalmatian dialects  dos Cano the Tuscan dialects that are  spoken in the tuscany region and in  particular the florentine dialect of  florence the romance dialects in italy  like all Romance languages developed  from varieties of vulgar latin latin  originally the language of Rome and the  Roman Empire remained the formal  language of writing and literature up  until the late Middle Ages in what is  now Italy but during that period a  vernacular literary tradition began to  emerge in other words literature written  in the language of everyday speech  rather than Latin became more widespread  the seeds of standard Italian were  planted by Tuscan poetry and literature  in the 13th century in the 14th century  Tuscan poet Dante Alighieri published  his masterpiece the Divine Comedy in  Italian originally called just comedia  but later referred to as Divina Commedia  Dante was from Florence and his writing  largely resembled the Tuscan dialect of  that city with some elements of Latin as  well as other dialects Dante is often  referred to as the father of the Italian  language because his work came to be  read by educated people in every region  of the peninsula as a result the form of  language used in his writing became the  most widely understood and over the next  couple of centuries it became  established as the de facto standard  literary language other 14th century  poets who greatly contributed to this  were Francesco Petrarca  and Giovanni Boccaccio in the 16th  century Pietro Benbow helped to  standardize literary Italian based on  the model of Petrarca Boccaccio and  allegory later in the 19th century a  novelist named Alessandro Manzoni  had a big impact on the development of  modern Standard Italian thanks to his  novel the betrothed witch  is called Ypres mrs. palsey in Italian  this was the first novel written in  Italian and is said to be the most  famous novel in the Italian language  even though it's not widely read in  other languages Manzoni wrote the novel  in the modern Florentine dialect with  some influence from the Florentine  literature of the 14th century when the  historical states of Italy became  unified in the 19th century Italian  became the official language of Italy  and the Italian government accepted  Manzo knees proposal to make this  variety standard Italian at the time of  unification most Italians were not able  to speak Italian  meaning literary Tuscan different  estimates from that time range from 2.5  percent of the population to 10 percent  of the population even though it was the  most widely understood written language  most of the population were illiterate  and lacked exposure to it unifying the  linguistically diverse nation became one  of italians main functions nowadays  virtually all Italians are fluent in  Italian which exists alongside Italy's  vast array of romance dialects though in  some areas Italian has largely replaced  local dialect what's Italian like first  let's look at a few basic words and  phrases hello ciao notice the letter C  here represents the soft sound that's  how it's pronounced before I or II  before a Oh or you it represents a hard  sound as in quanto Costa which means how  much does it cost  good morning Buon Giorno notice here  that the letter G represents a soft  sound again that's the pronunciation  before I or II before a oh you or a  consonant it's pronounced gah  like in the word for thank you gasps  yeah aside from the hard G notice the  pronunciation of the letter Z in Italian  it represents a sound or at the  beginning of words though there are  exceptions goodbye  arrivederci or ciao note the  pronunciation of the Italian R sound  it's a rolled R sound in other words a  trill it's a longer trill sound when  doubled and it's a tap when it appears  between vowels you'll notice it in the  next couple of words as well  please bit of  I'd like Buddha I'd like the fish please  very delicious therefore water notice  the pronunciation of SC in baseship it's  pronounced like sure before I and II but  before other vowels or before consonants  it's pronounced scut like in the phrase  meaning excuse me miss scusi on top of  the sky sound notice that s is  pronounced Z between vowels  it's voiced where is the station no they  lost at SUNY recall that the letter Z  sounds like sir not Z I love Italy Amol  Italia I understand  capisco I don't understand non capisco  now let's jump into some more sentences  and see what we can learn about the  language marco parla Italiano english  this means mark speaks english and  italian word for word it Marco speaks  Italian and English in simple sentences  like this the word order is the same as  in English another thing you might  notice and you may have noticed in the  previous set of basic words and phrases  is the strong tendency for Italian words  to end in a vowel that's true for almost  all words in Italian they end in vowels  we saw two exceptions in the previous  set of basic words Ile meaning da and no  no meaning no or not both of them short  function words there are five vowel  letters in Italian ah ay ay e oo o mmm  two of them E and O have two sounds open  and closed it's worth noting that  different regional accents don't always  distinguish between the open and closed  vowel sounds so if you can't it's not  the end of the world I guess there are  also dipped on though they are usually  just a simple combination of two vowels  another thing to notice is that each of  the words with more than one syllable is  stressed on the penultimate syllable in  other words the second to last syllable  Marco parla Italiano  English this isn't true for all words in  Italian but it's generally the case the  verb  Bala is the third person singular  conjugation of the verb para la in the  present tense Italian has three classes  of verbs are verbs  err err verbs and ei verbs notice that  the three classes are conjugated  slightly differently with some slightly  different verb endings other verbs are  almost all regular but among err a and E  reverbs there's a significant number of  irregular verbs that veer from the  regular patterns let's look at another  sentence oh man Giotto in your cheapo  periphery topper pranzo gee lasagna  it means I ate my favourite food for  lunch today lasagne word-for-word it  I have eaten the my food favorite for  lunch today lasagna I guess I didn't  really need to translate lasagna in this  sentence the syntax is a little  different from English unlike the  previous sentence first of all the  subject pronoun can be dropped because  the person and number are clear from the  conjugation of the verb Oh is the first  person singular present tense form of  the verb  Avira which means to have here it's  functioning as the auxiliary verb for  man giotto this is the tense passat  o'brosey mou equivalent to the present  perfect tense in English but in Italian  it's also generally used where we would  use the simple past in English this is  one of 14 tenses in Italian though there  are 21 if we include the imperative  present and past infinitives present and  past participles and present and past  gerunds some of those tenses are simple  tenses and others are compound tenses  formed from an auxiliary verb plus the  past participle of the main verb or the  body gpo bus Sato for most verbs  compound tenses are formed with the  auxiliary verb avere though there is a  subset of verbs that take s city meaning  to be instead these are usually  intransitive verbs that describe  movement or a state or condition let's  look at the phrase il mio Cibo mio is a  possessive adjective like my in English  the possessive adjectives as well as  other adjectives like prefer Ito  agree with the gender and number of the  noun nouns ending in O are usually  masculine like Tchibo and nouns ending  in a are usually feminine like torta  meaning cake some nouns end in E and  these can be either masculine or  feminine  for instance panty meaning bread is mass  given while Karen a meaning meat is  feminine in Italian possessive  adjectives are preceded by a definite  article like neo Chivo  la mia torta mei biscotti lemmya  rejected notice that there are different  definite articles that agree with gender  and number masculine and feminine  singular and masculine and feminine  plural there are actually a couple more  that appear in certain phonetic contexts  low masculine singular before s plus  another consonant or before Z look l '  both masculine and feminine singular  before vowels and masculine plural  before vowels before s plus another  consonant or the consonant Z or before  GN so there's L Cibo lewd zucchero  la niñas la torta EB Scott the learjet  de Jean Yawkey notice that the  adjectives prefer Ito comes after the  noun whereas an English it comes before  it in Italian functional adjectives like  possessive adjectives and numbers come  before the noun descriptive adjectives  normally come after the now but they  sometimes come before it  the general rule is that adjectives that  are strictly factual come after the now  these are called denoted adjectives for  example la torta fresca the fresh cake  fresh is denoted it's factual adjectives  that are connotative in other words they  imply something about the noun come  before  for example la deliciosa torta the  delicious cake some adjectives can come  before or after but with a difference in  meaning for example wawapo vero means  the poor man he's factually poor while  in Barbara warm means the miserable man  a couple of notes about pronunciation  and orthography Oh  note that the letter H is silent in  Italian in the phrase y'know we see two  different consonant sounds that are  written with G plus another consonant  letter G L represents the sound Lea this  is one of the trickier sounds for  learners of Italian it's kind of like  the double L sound in the word million  but not quite G n represents the sound  a palatal nasal something like yeah but  not quite  also note the CH in Miyake and also in  zucchero CH is always pronounced as a  hard sound in Italian similarly G H is  always pronounced as a hard sound  puggy d Mundi another sentence lays  Tendo Sanibel Amanita this means you're  wearing a nice shirt work forward it's  you stay wearing aa nice shirt this  sentence could also mean she is wearing  a nice shirt this is a polite sentence  and in polite speech third-person  pronouns and conjugations are used for  the second person lait is the third  person feminine singular subject pronoun  and lay with a capital L is the polite  second person singular but lay would be  capitalized here anyway since it's at  the beginning of the sentence so out of  context the sentence is ambiguous but  not in context Stein dose and O is a  present continuous construction it uses  the verb study as the auxiliary verb and  the gerund of the main verb here the  verb is endo sorry because this is a  polite sentence study is in its third  person singular conjugation stop rather  than second person singular notice also  the indefinite article here indefinite  articles agree with the gender of the  noun  una is the feminine indefinite article  and it agrees with the feminine noun  Murrieta though before vowels the  feminine article is une with an  apostrophe  una Inca the masculine definite article  is una as in une Capello though it's una  before Z or s with another consonant uno  studio there are no plural indefinite  articles but partitive articles meaning  some or any are used instead positive  articles combine deep meaning of with a  definite article as we saw before this  adjective here also agrees with the noun  and gender as well as in number then la  is the feminine singular form it comes  before the noun because it's connotative  it's not factual  but implies something subjective about  the shirt Italian is a beautiful  language and one that captures the  imagination of people all over the world  because of the huge Italian cultural  influence both historical and  contemporary I guess that's why Italian  is the fourth most widely studied  language in the world some sources say  5th but let's go with 4th the question  of the day for native speakers of  Italian what other features of Italian  do you want others to know about and for  learners of Italian what have you found  the most fun or interesting about the  language if you enjoyed this video then  check out Lange focus on the various  social media platforms like Facebook  Instagram Twitter and of course YouTube  and as always I want to give a very  special thanks to my patrons on patreon  especially my top 2 your patrons whose  names are right here on the screen many  extra special thanks to them and to  everyone thank you for watching and have  a nice day  [Music]  you  [Music]

